  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nObservations this evening show temperatures have already dropped\ninto the 20s under rising pressures and strong radiational\ncooling. This is setting up for a chilly start to the\nThanksgiving holiday, as temperatures will start the day in the\n20s and even a few spots in the teens. Temperatures will remain\ncool throughout the day, remaining largely in the 40s, some 3-7\ndegrees below climatological normals. Winds will be light across\nthe entire forecast area and there will be minimal clouds to\nspeak of, so weather is not expected to be a factor in any\nThanksgiving festivities. High temperatures will be back on the\nrise into Black Friday, into the 60s, with northwesterly winds \nfavoring at least some warming downslope flow.\n\nThe next chance for impactful weather will be in the form of a\nlow pressure system for this weekend. A potent shortwave,\ncurrently situated well into the Pacific, is expected to come\nashore along the Pacific Northwest Friday evening. The system\nwill promote surface cyclogenesis across southeast CO,\nPanhandles, and northwest OK Saturday. The most impactful\nportion of this quick hitting system is looking be the \npotential for strong winds. Latest global deterministic\nsolutions show at least 45 mph gust in the immediate wake of \nthe cold frontal passage into Friday evening, with some more \nbullish solutions reaching 60 mph or greater. The idea of higher\nchances of impactful wind is also evident on ensembles and the \nNBM as probabilities for 50 mph wind gusts are greater are \nappearing in various solutions. In fact, only 24 hours ago the \nNBM was not showing any chances, but now is beginning to \nhighlight western zones. The NBM mean is likely not strong \nenough and will need upward adjustments as we get closer. \nWhether this event could reach high wind criteria is uncertain, \nbut nonetheless, it is increasingly probable any post holiday \ntravel could be disrupted.\n\nRegarding precipitation chances for this system, NBM POPs have\nincreased across areas north of Route 50, especially up along\nthe I-70 corridor. Rain and/or snow could occur as indicated by\nconsensus from models of a deformation zone trying to develop \nacross central Kansas. Some model point soundings in the\npotential precipitation zone suggest a quick changeover from\nrain to snow. However, uncertainty still remains on how quickly\nthat colder air can filter in immediately behind the system. If\na changeover to snow could occur, with those strong winds, some\nadditional travel disruptions would be on the table. We will \nneed to continue to monitor progression among the guidance on \nthis evolution. The feel from this forecaster and previous \ncontinue to think the pattern is too progressive for anything \nsignificantly impactful. The most likely scenario at this time\nseems to be initial rain with snow trying to filter in, but not\namount to much if any. In any case, as stated previously, the\nstrong wind is the highest confidence to the forecast. The\nconfidence in precipitation type and amount is low.",
